          bodyZh: `12.3.3.2 Morphological processing
· Psycholinguistic research reveals that like phonemic units, morphological structures also play a substantial role in the representation of words in the mind and in word recognition.
· In the recognition of multimorphemic words (compound words, derivational words and inflectional words), which are composed of two or more morphemes, individual morphemes are automatically activated.
· Priming experiments show that when a subject is exposed to a multimorphemic word such as boyish, the activation of that word in the mind automatically activates the lexical entry for its root boy, which results in its showing a faster response time as the target in a lexical decision task.
· They also show that the presentation of a compound such as bigwig will produce a priming effect for the subsequent lexical decision time for a word such as hair.
· For this priming effect to take place, the morpheme wig would have to be individually activated during word recognition because, as a whole, the compound bigwig has nothing to do with hair.
· Selectional restrictions.
– In English morphology, there are some restrictions when affixes are attached to stems.
– For example, the suffix -en can be attached to a monosyllabic adjective that ends in a stop, a fricative or an affricate to form verbs such as darken, stiffen and richen, but not to disyllabic or multisyllabic words or monosyllabic words that do not end in a stop, a fricative or an affricate.
– Research shows that knowledge of such selectional restrictions of affixes does indeed form part of the word-processing system.
– For example, in an experiment in morphological processing, subjects were presented with nonsense roots such as darge (roots which do not exist) that had prefixes and suffixes attached to them such as en-darge and en-darge-ment.
– Some of the words presented are morphologically permissible while others are impermissible.
– It was found that response times were significantly longer for the impermissible nonsense words than for the morphologically permissible words.
· Hierarchical structure.
– In English, multimorphemic words are not formed by attaching all the affixes to a root at a time.
– For different words, affixes are added in different order.
– For example, the word refillable is derived by first attaching the prefix re- and then adding the suffix -able to the root fill, while the word uneatable is formed by first attaching the suffix -able to eat and then adding the prefix un-.
– That is to say, multimorphemic words have hierarchical structures.
– However, does the representation of such words in the mind also represent such structures?
– Priming experiments have been used to investigate this question.
– In the experiments, stimuli such as refillable and uneatable, which are formed in different orders, are presented.
– Subjects were first shown the substrings with a suffix, such as fillable and eatable, and then the full trimorphemic words, such as re-fill-able and un-eat-able.
– Priming effects were measured in terms of the lexical decision response times for the full word.
– It was found that real morphological constituents like eatable produced a significantly larger priming effect than those that were non-words like fillable.
– The result shows that our representation of complex words is organized in terms of a hierarchical morphological structure.`,
