          titleZh: "4.3 导语",
          bodyZh: `4.3 Conversion
· Definition
– Formation of new words by converting words of one class to another class.
– Turning words of one part of speech to those of another part of speech.
– New words are new only in a grammatical sense.
– Words do not change in morphological structure but in function.
– Also known as functional shift.
· Example: round
– "He was knocked out in the first round." (noun)
– "Round the number off to the nearest tenth." (verb)
– "The neighbours gathered round our barbecue." (preposition)
– "The moon was bright and round." (adjective)
– "People came from all the country round." (adverb)
· Also known as zero-derivation
– Item adapted or converted to new word class without addition of an affix.
– Example: single and simple
◦ Both adjectives.
◦ single can be used as verb as it is:
▪ "She singled him out at once as a possible victim."
◦ simple cannot function as verb without adding an affix:
▪ "Continental quilts simplify (not simple) bed-making."
◦ First instance: zero-derivation–conversion.
◦ Second: suffixation as -ify added.
· Words produced by conversion
– Mainly nouns, adjectives, and verbs.
– Most productive: conversion between nouns and verbs.
· Note
– Conversion is not only change of grammatical function.
– With it the different range of meaning it originally carried.
– Example: paper as noun has four senses:
◦ material in thin sheets
◦ a newspaper
◦ a piece of writing for specialists
◦ wallpaper
– When turned into transitive verb, related only to (4):
◦ "She papered the room green."`,
